 notes="Typed page 15 gives the characteristic-three and characteristic-two normal forms and states Proposition 6.9 on automorphisms. It contains the handwritten objection ‘faux si S / pas connexe’ (pas uncertain) beside (I), and ‘structure ?’ beside the order 24 in (V). H reads non instead of pas, but the initial descender supports p. The circle at the stop after corps and the large loop around 24 are not deletions. sour, résoud, d’ur, the references (6.3)/(6.4), and the tag (6.6.) are retained as printed. The first two references appear to point to (5.3)/(5.4) on scan 25.\n\nS030-I-LITERAL: The marginal objection identifies a real problem if (I) is read as exactly two global automorphisms over every S. Take S as two disjoint copies of Spec(C) and the curve y²=x³+x+1 on each. Its c_4=−48, c_6=−864 and Δ=−496 are nonzero. The automorphism (x,y)↦(x,−y) on one component and the identity on the other preserves the equation and is neither the global identity nor global inversion. This refutes that literal global reading. S030-I-REFINED: At least componentwise choices must be allowed; this check does not claim a full classification on arbitrary nonreduced bases. The remaining group assertions are transcribed as claims, with the author’s ‘structure ?’ still open.",
